<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Prince</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1513</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Il Principe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Prince is a political treatise by the Florentine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>, written in 1513 after the return of the Medici had cost him his office, and published in 1532. Dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, Duke of Urbino, it describes how new princes win and hold power, arguing that a ruler must sometimes act against conventional morality. Its frank realism made it one of the most influential and controversial works of political thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-prince.jpg" alt="The Prince" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niccolò Machiavelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolo_Machiavelli,_Il_Principe,_1550,_title_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
